<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

    <!-- ===== matomo-java-tracker =====
         CVE-2023-6923: Targets the Matomo PHP web analytics application, not the Java client.
         False positive — CPE matches on product name "matomo"/"piwik" regardless of ecosystem. -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            matomo-java-tracker-core-3.0.6.jar: CVE-2023-6923 affects the Matomo PHP server,
            not the Java tracker client. False positive due to CPE/package name overlap.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.piwik\.java\.tracking/.*@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2023-6923</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!-- ===== opentelemetry-semconv (Java) =====
         CVE-2026-39882, CVE-2026-39883: Both CVEs target the OpenTelemetry Go SDK
         (go.opentelemetry.io/otel), fixed in Go SDK v1.43.0. They do not apply to the
         Java Maven artifact io.opentelemetry.semconv:opentelemetry-semconv. The scanner
         conflates Go SDK version numbers with the Java semconv artifact version numbers.
         1.41.1 is the latest Java release and is not affected. -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            False positive: CVE-2026-39882 and CVE-2026-39883 describe vulnerabilities in the
            OpenTelemetry Go SDK (memory exhaustion via unbounded OTLP HTTP response body, and
            BSD kenv PATH hijacking), not in the Java semconv conventions library. NVD's CPE
            matching hits io.opentelemetry.semconv because the product name "opentelemetry"
            matches both ecosystems. No Java fix exists or is needed.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/io\.opentelemetry\.semconv/.*@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-39882</cve>
    </suppress>
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            False positive: see CVE-2026-39882 note above. Same Go-only advisory, same
            incorrect CPE match against the Java semconv artifact.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/io\.opentelemetry\.semconv/.*@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-39883</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!-- ===== co.elastic.clients (Java REST API clients: elasticsearch-java, elasticsearch-rest5-client) =====
         NVD matches the Elasticsearch *server* CPEs (cpe:2.3:a:elastic:elasticsearch and
         cpe:2.3:a:elasticsearch:elasticsearch) against these co.elastic.clients artifacts
         purely because Elastic ships the clients with the same version number as the server
         they target. Both are REST API wrappers with no native code, no ML inference engine,
         and no server-side components, so server-only CVEs cannot apply to them.
         dependency-check requires an exact (non-wildcard) CPE match for <cpe> suppression
         rules, so each affected client version needs its own <suppress> entry here when server CVEs get
         misattributed to these jars. Add one per CVE and client version when that
         next happens rather than reaching for a blanket <cpe> wildcard, which the plugin
         silently ignores (zero matches). -->

    <!-- ===== org.springframework.ai (all spring-ai-* 1.1.8 artifacts) =====
         CVE-2026-59318: prompt-injection authorization bypass in DefaultToolCallingManager
         (a tool call not advertised to the current request can still be dispatched). Only
         reachable if the app wires up Spring AI's chat-client/tool-calling support. This
         project only pulls spring-ai-starter-model-transformers (local ONNX embedding
         transformers, transitively via registry-starter-service) for embeddings/search
         similarity — no ChatClient or tool-calling starter is on the classpath, so the
         vulnerable code path is not reachable. Spring's own advisory
         (spring.io/security/cve-2026-59318) rates this 6.5 MEDIUM (AC:H, PR:L, UI:R,
         requires an authenticated tool-calling flow); NVD's CVSS of 9.8 CRITICAL used by
         this scanner has a mismatched vector (AC:L, PR:N, UI:N) that does not match the
         vendor's own analysis. NVD's CPE (cpe:2.3:a:vmware:spring_ai:1.1.8) matches the
         whole 1.1.8 release rather than just the spring-ai-model artifact that actually
         contains DefaultToolCallingManager, so every spring-ai-* 1.1.8 jar on the classpath
         (model, commons, template-st, transformers, autoconfigure-*, ...) gets flagged —
         hence the groupId-wide regex below instead of one entry per artifact. Public OSS
         fix is 2.0.1 (major version bump); the 1.1.x-line backport (1.1.9) exists only
         under HeroDevs paid Enterprise support, not on Maven Central. Revisit when a public
         1.1.x fix ships or if this app starts using Spring AI tool calling. -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            CVE-2026-59318: prompt-injection tool-dispatch bypass in DefaultToolCallingManager.
            Not reachable — this app only uses spring-ai-starter-model-transformers (local
            embedding transformers) via registry-starter-service; no ChatClient/tool-calling
            starter is present. Vendor CVSS is 6.5 MEDIUM; NVD's 9.8 CRITICAL score used by
            dependency-check has a mismatched attack vector versus Spring's own advisory. No
            public (non-Enterprise) fix exists for the 1.1.x line as of 2026-09-17; fixed
            upstream in 2.0.1, a major version bump not otherwise compatible with the other
            spring-ai-* 1.1.8 artifacts pinned by registry-starter-service. Applies to every
            spring-ai-* 1.1.8 artifact since NVD's CPE match is release-wide, not per-module.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework\.ai/.*@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-59318</cve>
    </suppress>

</suppressions>
